ZIG-MAN-INSTALL(7)					    Zig Manual					       ZIG-MAN-INSTALL(7)

NAME
       zig-man-install - installation guide for zig-man man pages

SYNOPSIS
       make install [PREFIX=/path] [DESTDIR=/path]
       make uninstall [PREFIX=/path] [DESTDIR=/path]

DESCRIPTION
       This  page  documents  how to install zig-man, a collection of manual pages for the Zig programming language, on Linux and
       other Unix-like systems.

       zig-man is distributed as source. Installation is performed by make(1) and consists of copying man source files	into  the
       system manual page hierarchy. No compilation step is required.

REQUIREMENTS
       The following tools are required at install time:

       make(1)
	      GNU Make or BSD Make. The provided Makefile is POSIX-compatible and works with both.

       install(1)
	      The BSD install utility, present in coreutils and BSD userlands.

       man(1) The manual page viewer. Usually provided by man-db on Linux or mandoc on BSD.

       mandb(8)
	      Optional. Used to update the manual page index after installation.  If absent, make install skips this step.

DEPENDENCIES
       The following tools are used by auxiliary targets. They are not required for installation.

       mandoc(1)
	      Used by make lint, make html, and make txt. Provides syntax validation and rendering to HTML and plain text.

       groff(1)
	      Used by make pdf. Provides the man macro package and PostScript output.

       ghostscript(1)
	      Provides ps2pdf(1), used as a fallback when groff cannot produce PDF directly.

       entr(1)
	      Used by make watch for live preview. If absent, inotifywait(1) is used instead.

       hunspell(1) or aspell(1)
	      Used by make spell for spell checking.

       Installation of these tools varies by distribution:
	   # Fedora / RHEL / CentOS
	   sudo dnf install mandoc groff ghostscript man-db entr \
			    inotify-tools hunspell

	   # Debian / Ubuntu
	   sudo apt install mandoc groff ghostscript man-db entr \
			    inotify-tools hunspell

	   # Arch Linux
	   sudo pacman -S mandoc groff ghostscript man-db entr \
			  inotify-tools hunspell

	   # Alpine Linux
	   sudo apk add mandoc groff ghostscript man-db entr \
			inotify-tools hunspell

BUILD FROM SOURCE
       Obtain the source tree and enter it:
	   git clone https://github.com/mroczect/zig-man
	   cd zig-man

       No build step is required. The manual pages are plain roff source.

INSTALLATION PATHS
       By default, pages are installed under /usr/local:
	   /usr/local/share/man/man1/	 commands
	   /usr/local/share/man/man3/	 library reference
	   /usr/local/share/man/man5/	 file formats
	   /usr/local/share/man/man7/	 topic overviews

       The  prefix  is	controlled by the PREFIX variable. The manual page directory is derived as $(PREFIX)/share/man and can be
       overridden directly with MANDIR.

       Install into a staging directory, for packaging or testing, with DESTDIR:
	   make install DESTDIR=/tmp/pkgroot

       This places files under /tmp/pkgroot/usr/local/share/man.

INSTALL
       Run:
	   make install

       To install under a custom prefix:
	   make install PREFIX=$HOME/.local

       This installs to $HOME/.local/share/man. Make sure this directory is in the manual page search path. See the MANPATH  sec‐
       tion below.

       To install with elevated privileges, either run as root or use sudo(8):
	   sudo make install

       The  install  target copies files with mode 0644 and creates destination directories as needed. After copying, it attempts
       to run mandb(8) if available.

POST-INSTALLATION
       After installation, verify that the pages are found:
	   man 7 zig-variables
	   man 7 zig-man-install
	   man 1 zig

       If the pages are not found, the manual page database may need to be updated:
	   sudo mandb

       On systems using mandoc as the primary viewer, the makewhatis(8) database is used instead. Consult the distribution  docu‐
       mentation for the appropriate command.

   MANPATH
       If pages are installed under a prefix not in the default search path, set MANPATH:
	   export MANPATH=$HOME/.local/share/man:$MANPATH

       Add this to the shell profile for persistence. On Linux, manpath(1) can be used to inspect the current search path:
	   manpath

VERIFICATION
       List installed files:
	   ls -R /usr/local/share/man/man{1,3,5,7}

       Check that each page parses correctly:
	   make lint

       Preview a page without installing:
	   make preview FILE=man/man7/zig-variables.7

       Render all pages to HTML, PDF, and plain text:
	   make html pdf txt

       Output is written to build/html, build/pdf, and build/txt respectively.

PACKAGING
       The Makefile supports DESTDIR for integration with package build systems. A typical packaging invocation is:
	   make install DESTDIR=$RPM_BUILD_ROOT PREFIX=/usr

       This places files under $RPM_BUILD_ROOT/usr/share/man. The FHS specifies /usr/share/man as the canonical location for sys‐
       tem manual pages; /usr/local/share/man is used for locally installed software.

       Do not run mandb during packaging. The package manager invokes it after installation.

TROUBLESHOOTING
   mandoc: not found
       Install mandoc:
	   sudo dnf install mandoc     # Fedora
	   sudo apt install mandoc     # Debian
	   sudo pacman -S mandoc       # Arch

   groff: fatal error: cannot load 'DESC' description file for device 'pdf'
       The groff installation is incomplete. Install ghostscript and retry:
	   sudo dnf install ghostscript

       The Makefile falls back to groff -Tps | ps2pdf when direct PDF output is unavailable.

   cannot parse date, using it verbatim
       The  .TH	 line  contains	 a  date  in a format mandoc does not recognize. Use ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD) or a month name (Month
       YYYY). Run make format-header to normalize dates automatically.

   missing: zig (referenced in ...)
       A page references another page that does not exist in the tree. Create the missing page, or  adjust  the	 cross-reference.
       make check reports these without failing; make check-strict fails.

   local variable is never mutated
       This  is a Zig compiler message, not an installation error. It indicates that a var declaration in the page source was not
       used. Manual pages are not compiled by Zig; if this message appears, it is being emitted by an unrelated tool.

UNINSTALL
       Remove installed pages:
	   sudo make uninstall

       With a custom prefix:
	   make uninstall PREFIX=$HOME/.local

       The uninstall target removes only the files that install would have copied. It does not remove the destination directories
       themselves.

FILES
       /usr/local/share/man/man1/
	      Default location for command pages.

       /usr/local/share/man/man3/
	      Default location for library reference pages.

       /usr/local/share/man/man5/
	      Default location for file format pages.

       /usr/local/share/man/man7/
	      Default location for topic overview pages.

       build/ Generated output from make html, make pdf, and make txt. Removed by make clean.

SEE ALSO
       make(1), install(1), man(1), man(7), man-pages(7), manpath(1), mandb(8), mandoc(1), groff(1), ps2pdf(1)

       Zig language reference: https://ziglang.org/documentation/

